      "title": "Privacy and what never goes in the box",
      "minutes": 8,
      "when": "Before you paste anything",
      "body": [
        "NIST’s Privacy Framework is a voluntary way for organizations to identify and reduce privacy risk. The personal version is short: do not hand over data the task does not need. That is data minimization.",
        "Do not paste classified information, CUI, medical records, Social Security numbers, bank numbers, passwords, or full personnel files. A wording tool does not need them. You cannot get a secret back once it has been sent to a service you do not control. This lesson is not a classification ruling. If you are unsure, leave the sentence out and ask your command through official channels.",
        "Commercial chat tools often keep prompts on the company’s servers. Read that company’s current policy if you use one. This toolkit’s pages do not include an account and do not send the form to a server. Do not assume another product works the same way.",
        "Screenshots can contain more than the sentence you noticed: a name in the corner, a phone number, a partial account balance. Crop tight, or type the sentence instead. Scam Guardian can read a screenshot with local OCR, and you should still remove unrelated details first.",
        "Family members helping an older veteran should use the same rule. Helping does not mean pasting someone else’s identity documents into a website. If information was already shared with a scammer, VA points to the bank and to IdentityTheft.gov. Do not pay the same caller a fee to fix it."
      ],
      "sources": [
        {"name": "NIST Privacy Framework", "href": NIST_PRIVACY},
        {"name": "VA, Protecting Veterans from Fraud", "href": VA_FRAUD},
        {"name": "IdentityTheft.gov", "href": IDTHEFT}
      ],
      "quiz": [
        {"q": "Data minimization means:", "choices": ["Pasting your whole record so the tool has context.", "Sharing only what the task needs.", "Posting duties on social media first.", "Emailing a roster to a chatbot to save time."], "answer": 1, "why": "NIST’s Privacy Framework is about managing privacy risk, including limiting data. A civilian-wording task does not need an identity number or a medical note.", "source": NIST_PRIVACY, "sourceName": "NIST Privacy Framework"},
        {"q": "Which paste is appropriate for a civilian-wording task?", "choices": ["A separation document image that includes a Social Security number.", "An unclassified duty paragraph with sensitive identifiers removed.", "A clinic note, so the tool can explain a claim.", "A list of other people’s home addresses from a roster."], "answer": 1, "why": "The task needs the duty, not identity documents, health notes, or other people’s private data. When a detail might be CUI or classified, leave it out.", "source": NIST_PRIVACY, "sourceName": "NIST Privacy Framework"},
        {"q": "What is different about the tools in this repository?", "choices": ["They file claims with VA automatically.", "They are built to run the lookup, the rules, the lessons, and the checklist in the browser after the files load.", "They store your record in a Foundation account.", "They are an official DoD system."], "answer": 1, "why": "The pages are static files with no account. A commercial chat product may store prompts under its own policy. Read that policy before you paste. Do not assume it matches these pages.", "source": NIST_PRIVACY, "sourceName": "NIST Privacy Framework"},
        {"q": "Why crop a screenshot before OCR?", "choices": ["OCR cannot read a full-width image.", "The edges often contain names, numbers, or balances you did not mean to process.", "Tesseract requires images under one inch tall.", "Cropping sends the image to a safer server."], "answer": 1, "why": "A screenshot is a picture of a whole screen. Scam Guardian’s OCR stays on the device, and you should still remove details the scam check does not need.", "source": VA_FRAUD, "sourceName": "VA, Protecting Veterans from Fraud"},
        {"q": "If you already gave an account number to a scammer, a sound next step is:", "choices": ["Pay the same caller a recovery fee.", "Contact the bank using a number you look up, and use IdentityTheft.gov if identity documents were exposed.", "Paste the account number into a chatbot to see if it is safe.", "Post the number so friends can check it."], "answer": 1, "why": "VA tells people who shared financial information to contact the bank, and points to IdentityTheft.gov for a recovery plan. A recovery fee demanded by the same party is another request to refuse.", "source": IDTHEFT, "sourceName": "IdentityTheft.gov"}
      ]
