        "VA says scammers pretend to be an agency or a person you know, push you to act immediately, and ask for payments that are hard to trace, including gift cards. VBA’s fraud-prevention page says VA does not request payment by gift card, wire, cryptocurrency, or a prepaid card. A threat to suspend benefits plus one of those payments is the tactic, not the polish of the writing.",
        "VA describes claims predators as people who unlawfully charge veterans to help with a claim and who promise speed or a higher rating. Use a VA-accredited attorney, claims agent, or Veterans Service Organization representative. Pension poaching, including advice to hide assets so someone appears to qualify, is described on VBA’s fraud page. You may have to repay benefits.",
        "Use Scam Guardian to see which written tactics are in a message you paste. Then report through channels you look up: ReportFraud.ftc.gov, IC3, and VA’s own fraud page. A low score is not a clearance. The page does not judge a voice, a face, or a video.",
        "This lesson does not give dollar totals or percentages for veteran fraud. Those figures change, and a wrong number helps nobody. The tactics above are the part you can act on today."
