        "Start with the code you held, not with a dream job title. The career tool shows the DMDC crosswalk distributed by the O*NET Resource Center, then O*NET 31.0 text for the linked occupation. O*NET is a Department of Labor product used here under CC BY 4.0. USDOL/ETA has not approved this shortened copy. Read the official title before you read any suggested civilian titles.",
        "Some codes, including many infantry codes, point at a military O*NET occupation in group 55. That means the crosswalk is describing the service job. A dictionary suggestion beside it is a writing idea from the Debriefed notes, not an announcement that an employer must hire you into that role.",
        "When you reword a duty, keep the true object of the work: people, equipment, schedules, safety, training, records. Swap jargon only when the civilian phrase still means what you did. A pay-grade table can suggest the scope of responsibility. It is not a GS appointment and it is not a civilian rank.",
        "Numbers are your evidence. If you do not know the number, leave a blank and look it up in your own records. Do not let a template fill it in. My Next Move for Veterans is a public place to explore civilian work after you have read the crosswalk. It is not a job offer.",
        "A chatbot can propose wording. CareerOneStop’s warning still applies: keep only what is true. Never paste classified information, CUI, or another person’s private data to get a smoother bullet."
